WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:18.440
When somebody walks onto the floor for the first time, the biggest shock is really that we say

00:18.440 --> 00:23.560
we're building prototypes and you see essentially almost a full manufacturing line. And that's

00:23.560 --> 00:29.580
really why prototype operations exist is because we are putting a level of scaled rigor into a

00:29.580 --> 00:35.400
prototype process. And that's another reason that allows us to do prototypes at scale, not just one or two.

00:35.400 --> 00:43.320
Prototype operations helps support research and development builds and our suite of services that

00:43.320 --> 00:48.600
can help translate everyone's designs into real products across the whole spectrum of being able

00:48.600 --> 00:52.620
to build it, test it, support it, and maintain it all within a prototype space.

00:52.620 --> 01:06.120
We own our own shop because we get so many no quotes. Nobody can do what we do. Every time we

01:06.120 --> 01:12.360
try to get something done outside, they say we can't do it. We work with such tight tolerances

01:12.360 --> 01:15.840
that it's very hard to get work done outside the shop.

01:15.840 --> 01:21.660
We're really trying to accelerate velocity of technology development. And so having our own

01:21.660 --> 01:27.780
precision shop allows us to both have the engineering team come down to the machine shop, interact, make

01:27.780 --> 01:32.160
adjustments really fast, and then produce the parts at extremely high quality and extreme speed.

01:36.660 --> 01:43.980
Machining parts for robots that don't exist yet varies from production because you're developing the

01:43.980 --> 01:50.280
machining method. You know, you're doing the first pass on the design for manufacturing where, you know,

01:50.280 --> 01:56.160
tool selection is really important to creating the part efficiently and cost effectively.

01:57.780 --> 02:02.740
So here at Boston Dynamics, you know, we do some very niche things to make the robot go.

02:03.120 --> 02:09.600
So we do a lot of gear cutting and that's very, very specific in the industry. So you don't see it in many

02:09.600 --> 02:16.500
places and you really get to explore it here. We're basically the candy store for 600 engineers.

02:16.500 --> 02:22.440
So every time they need something, we have to come up with a way to make it. So every machine you see

02:22.440 --> 02:29.760
here is new. We've gotten more advanced year after year. We get to use a lot of different specialized

02:29.760 --> 02:36.780
equipment and tools. I could name an endless number that are really awesome where I would dream of having

02:36.780 --> 02:41.460
this at any other place I've ever worked. And here we just casually have it, which is phenomenal.

02:41.460 --> 02:47.400
Our precision shop downstairs has state of the art machining equipment on the line. We've got robot

02:47.400 --> 02:53.220
arms integrated into our assembly process. And a lot of this stuff is really phenomenal, but I think the

02:53.760 --> 02:59.160
coolest part of all of it is the collection of our capabilities and that we're constantly striving to

02:59.160 --> 03:06.240
add more and more capabilities. A year ago, building one robot was quite an event. So over the past year,

03:06.240 --> 03:12.240
we've really scaled up and now to build one robot, it's about a day. And next year, it's going to be

03:12.240 --> 03:16.920
about an hour. And then years after that, it's going to be minutes. The feedback loop is pretty brutal,

03:16.920 --> 03:22.500
but in a good way. You can go from having an idea Monday to being able to implement it by the end of

03:22.500 --> 03:28.200
the week. This spans across design, assembly processes, just general organizational processes,

03:28.200 --> 03:34.000
are allowed to rapidly iterate and find issues really, really fast and fail fast. And it allows us to be

03:34.000 --> 03:36.640
able to solve the right problems really quickly.

03:41.880 --> 03:47.020
They know we can make anything, so they're going to try anything. So when engineers come in here and

03:47.020 --> 03:52.320
they see that our capacities and see what we can do, they start really thinking outside the box. And

03:52.320 --> 03:55.600
that's what this whole company is about, thinking outside the box.

03:55.600 --> 04:02.320
I'd say the biggest game changer from general manufacturing is that here at Boston Dynamics, you

04:02.320 --> 04:08.620
get end-to-end ownership of a project. So you start with, you know, working with the engineers, trying to

04:08.620 --> 04:14.140
figure out the best way to approach any given project. And really develop it from start to finish.

04:14.140 --> 04:19.480
When you get to take one of the parts that you made and put it in a robot, it is awesome. One of the big

04:19.480 --> 04:24.460
differences between here and general machining in the, you know, most of the world is, you know,

04:24.460 --> 04:28.480
you're just making a part because it came down the order and you have no idea what it goes through.

04:28.480 --> 04:33.520
You know, maybe somebody tells you to go to a ship far away that you'll never be on, but you know,

04:33.520 --> 04:38.320
here we'll, we'll make a part. And then in some cases that parts assemble same day or next day,

04:38.320 --> 04:43.600
and then you can go upstairs into the humanoid department and, you know, watch it move. And it's

04:43.600 --> 04:50.140
really cool. You want to do something different every day, you want to have fun at work. It's a

04:50.140 --> 04:55.660
great place to work. Benefits are great. People are great. We work with the engineers and that's

04:55.660 --> 04:59.920
totally different. You never get to work with engineers anywhere else that I've been or seen,

04:59.920 --> 05:04.640
where you can talk to the engineer if you don't like what they did and they come back and they help

05:04.640 --> 05:09.700
you out. That's, that's a different world here. We get a lot of pressure, but we put a lot of pressure

05:09.700 --> 05:14.140
on ourselves. We get to do some of the coolest things in the world. In my opinion, we get to build

05:14.140 --> 05:19.060
robots. A lot of people get numb to it because you see robots walking around here every day, but it's

05:19.060 --> 05:24.660
extremely rewarding to be able to contribute to assembling something that you see operate either

05:24.660 --> 05:31.540
in a factory, you see it do a backflip on the Atlas lab. It takes an army of people to design and build

05:31.540 --> 05:37.220
and test these highly integrated systems. And I think that's just, it's just fun to be a part of.

05:39.700 --> 05:41.760
you
